WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.647
Memory came up briefly back in Module 9.

2
00:00:01.647 --> 00:00:07.000
Now the full picture — three scopes, how facts get in, and the budget that stops memory from crowding out the conversation itself.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.668
Three scopes.

4
00:00:07.668 --> 00:00:09.724
Chat memory belongs to one conversation.

5
00:00:09.724 --> 00:00:12.757
Project memory is shared by every chat inside that project.

6
00:00:12.757 --> 00:00:16.047
Global memory follows you everywhere, in every chat and project.

7
00:00:16.047 --> 00:00:18.000
Same mechanism, three different reach.

8
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.009
Facts get in two ways.

9
00:00:19.009 --> 00:00:23.596
Passively, the app quietly extracts them from what you say — that is the toggle in Settings, Memory.

10
00:00:23.596 --> 00:00:26.716
Or deliberately, when you say “remember that” and mean it literally.

11
00:00:26.716 --> 00:00:28.000
Both land in the same store.

12
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.160
Settings, Memory lists every fact by scope.

13
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.566
Delete a wrong one directly.

14
00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:39.000
And Tidy merges near-duplicates — the same fact saved three slightly different ways collapses back into one, which matters once the budget comes in.

15
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:46.618
The budget: memory is allowed twenty percent of the context window by default, twenty-five percent at the most, and that ceiling is hard-capped by design — it cannot be raised past it.

16
00:00:46.618 --> 00:00:52.000
The consequence: a smaller model recalls less, not because it forgets more, but because the budget is a share of a smaller window.

17
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.354
Gotcha: memory is not knowledge.

18
00:00:53.354 --> 00:00:56.146
A style preference — “always answer in bullet points” — is memory.

19
00:00:56.146 --> 00:00:59.615
A two-hundred-page spec is knowledge, and belongs in a knowledge base, not memory.

20
00:00:59.615 --> 00:01:03.000
Drop a whole document into memory and it can blow the entire budget on one file.

21
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.324
Memory keeps facts across chats.

22
00:01:04.324 --> 00:01:09.000
Next we look at chats themselves — folders, snapshots, branching, and how compaction keeps a long history usable.

